.. note::

   ``CVode.event()`` can be used to schedule an action to occur at a specific time, which can be useful for allowing time for events to affect their targets before stopping a simulation. Example usage:

   .. code-block:: none

      WAIT = 8
      proc counter() {
        stv.append(t)  // record time of source event
        if (stv.size() == MAXNUM) cvode.event(t + WAIT, "stoprun = 1")
      }

   This code allows 8 ms for the source event to be delivered and affect its target. ``CVode.event()`` can also call a function or procedure, enabling actions like printing spike times:

   .. code-block:: none

      proc wrapup() {
        print "spike times:"
        stv.printf
        stoprun = 1
      }

      proc counter() {
        stv.append(t)  // record time of source event
        if (stv.size() == MAXNUM) cvode.event(t + WAIT, "wrapup()")
      }

``CVode.event()`` can be used to schedule events that trigger procedural changes during a simulation. For example, it can be used to change parameters such as the amplitude of a stimulus or the weight of a NetCon at specific times. This is done by scheduling an event with ``cvode.event(time, "proc_name()")`` where ``proc_name`` is a procedure that executes the desired changes. If the parameter change affects a kinetic scheme or an ODE, it may be necessary to call ``cvode.re_init()`` to ensure the solver's internal state is consistent. Example:

.. code-block:: none

    objref fih
    fih = new FInitializeHandler("initi()")

    proc initi() {
      cvode.event(START, "seti()")
    }

    proc seti() {
      stim.amp = AMP
      cvode.event(t + DUR, "seti()")
      cvode.event(t + INTERVAL, "seti()")
    }

.. note::

   STATE variables that are defined as arrays are automatically treated as RANGE variables in NEURON. This means they are accessible from HOC or Python without needing to be explicitly declared as RANGE variables in the NEURON block. For example, if a STATE variable is defined as an array in NMODL, it can be accessed in HOC using the syntax ``statename_suffix[index](range)``. This allows for easy access and manipulation of STATE variables during simulations without the need for additional RANGE declarations.

   Example:

   .. code-block:: none

       NEURON {
         SUFFIX cadifus
         DEFINE Nannuli 4
       }

       STATE {
         ca[Nannuli] (mM) <1e-10>
       }

   In this example, the STATE variable ``ca`` is defined as an array with 4 elements. Each element can be accessed in HOC as ``ca_cadifus[0](x)``, ``ca_cadifus[1](x)``, etc., where ``x`` is the range value.

.. code-block:: none

   COMMENT
   isin.mod
   Delivers sinusoidal current i = amp*sin(f*(t - del) + phi)
   starting at t==del and ending at t==del+dur
   where f is frequency in radians/s and phi is phase in radians
   3/13/2009 NTC
   ENDCOMMENT

   NEURON {
     POINT_PROCESS Isin
     RANGE del, dur, f, phi, amp, i
     ELECTRODE_CURRENT i
   }

   UNITS {
     (nA) = (nanoamp)
     PI = (pi) (1)
     (Radian) = (1)
   }

   PARAMETER {
     del (ms)           : time at which current starts
     dur (ms) <0, 1e9>  : duration
     f (Radian/s) <0, 1e9> : frequency in radians/s
     phi (Radian)       : phase shift in radians
     amp (nA)
   }

   ASSIGNED {
     i (nA)
     on (1)
   }

   INITIAL {
     i = 0
     on = 0
     net_send(del, 1)
   }

   FUNCTION fsin(t (ms)) {
     fsin = sin(f*(t - del)/(1000) + phi)
   }

   BREAKPOINT {
     i = on*amp*fsin(t)
   }

   NET_RECEIVE (w) {
     if (flag == 1) {
       if (on == 0) {
         on = 1
         net_send(dur, 1)
       } else {
         on = 0
       }
     }
   }

.. note::

   ``proc segrun()`` is a procedure to execute a long simulation as a series of shorter segments. This approach helps manage memory usage by recording data in intervals that fit into memory. The procedure involves setting up Vector recording for each segment and appending results to a cumulative record.

   Example usage:

   .. code-block:: none

      proc segrun() { local i, interval, count, t2, lastpass
        localobj segtvec, tvec, tmpvec, isynvecs, segsumabsisyn, sumabsisyn
        // Setup recording Vectors and Lists
        // Main computational loop
        while (t<$1) {
          // Execute simulation segment
          // Process and store results
        }
      }

.. note::

   ``proc postproc()`` is a user-defined procedure intended for post-simulation analysis. It operates on recorded variables to derive additional data such as derivatives, integrals, or spike frequency. This procedure should be called after the simulation ends and can be customized to include various analyses. Example usage:

   .. code-block:: none

       proc postproc() {
         netca = integr(i_ca_soma, tvec, t0, t1)
         soma netca *= area(0.5)*(1e-8)
         // Additional analysis code
       }

   Ensure that ``postproc()`` is verified to work correctly before integrating it into the simulation workflow.

.. note::

   ``printf`` statements can be used inside BREAKPOINT and FUNCTION blocks in NMODL source code for debugging purposes. The syntax is identical to that used by C. Variable names should be used as they are known inside the NMODL source code without suffix or dot notation.

   Example:

   .. code-block:: none

       NEURON {
         POINT_PROCESS PRtest
         RANGE index
       }
       ASSIGNED {
         v (mV)
       }
       PARAMETER {
         index = 0
       }
       BREAKPOINT {
         printf("BREAKPOINT\t index %d\t, t %g,\t v %g\n", index, t, v)
       }
       AFTER BREAKPOINT {
         printf("AFTER BREAKPOINT\t index %d\t, t %g,\t v %g\n", index, t, v)
       }

.. note::

   ``SectionList`` can be used to collect sections based on naming patterns. For example, to collect all sections with 'dend' in their name:

   .. code-block:: none

       objref dendriticSections
       dendriticSections = new SectionList()
       forsec "dend" {
           dendriticSections.append()
       }

   This allows iteration over the collected sections:

   .. code-block:: none

       forsec dendriticSections {
           // perform operations on each section
       }

.. note::

   ``forsec`` can be used with a ``SectionList`` to iterate over specific sections. For example:

   .. code-block:: none

       objref dendriticSections
       dendriticSections = new SectionList()
       forsec "dend" {
           dendriticSections.append()
       }

       forsec dendriticSections {
           // perform operations on each section
       }

   This allows for efficient operations on a subset of sections.

.. note::

   Custom functions can be defined and used within ``forsec`` loops to perform calculations on each section:

   .. code-block:: none

       func myfunc() {
           return diam
       }

       forsec dendriticSections {
           print myfunc()
       }

   This allows for dynamic calculations based on section properties.

.. note::

   ``SectionList`` does not support direct indexing to retrieve specific sections. To iterate over specific groups of sections, use an auxiliary variable to track the index within a loop. For example:

   .. code-block:: none

       ii = 0
       forsec seclist {
         if (ii < 10) {
           statement1
         }
         if ((ii >= 10) && (ii < 20)) {
           statement2
         }
         ii += 1
       }

   This approach allows for executing different statements based on the index of the section within the list.

.. note::

   Currents declared in the ``WRITE`` clause of ``USEION`` statements must be in units of mA/cm2. If other units are needed, declare a surrogate variable in the ``NEURON`` block and assign it the desired units in the ``ASSIGNED`` block. Use a scale factor in the ``BREAKPOINT`` block to ensure consistency. For example:

   .. code-block::

       NEURON {
           USEION ca WRITE ica
           RANGE i
       }

       ASSIGNED {
           ica (mA/cm2)
           i (nA/cm2)
       }

       BREAKPOINT {
           ica = expression_that_returns_mA_cm2
           i = ica * (1e6)
       }

   Always check with ``modlunit`` to ensure no units inconsistencies.

.. note::

    In NMODL, the surface area of a segment can be accessed directly using the ``area`` variable.
    Declare ``area (micron2)`` in the ASSIGNED block and use it in any equation block.
    This provides the segment area without needing to calculate it manually using ``L`` and ``diam``.

    Example:

    .. code-block:: none

        ASSIGNED {
            area (micron2)
        }

        BREAKPOINT {
            surface_area = area
        }

    Remember to test your mod files with modlunit and use conversion factors as necessary to reconcile unit inconsistencies.
